WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.745
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:09.141
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.441 --> 00:00:20.638
Unit fifty-five: Cities of the Future (Speculative). In this unit, you will imagine future cities using precise collocation and connotation.

4
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:22.779
floating city.

5
00:00:23.479 --> 00:00:27.464
floating city means a city built on platforms on the sea or a lake.

6
00:00:27.764 --> 00:00:30.588
A floating city could rise with the sea level.

7
00:00:31.288 --> 00:00:34.277
Your turn. Say: floating city.

8
00:00:36.977 --> 00:00:37.989
floating city.

9
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:40.077
arcology.

10
00:00:40.777 --> 00:00:46.480
arcology means a huge single building designed to contain a whole ecological city.

11
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.299
An arcology would house a hundred thousand people under one roof.

12
00:00:51.999 --> 00:00:55.093
Your turn. Say: arcology.

13
00:00:57.793 --> 00:00:58.910
arcology.

14
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:01.138
megastructure.

15
00:01:01.838 --> 00:01:05.219
megastructure means an extremely large built structure.

16
00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.771
Saudi Arabia's The Line was planned as a vast megastructure.

17
00:01:10.471 --> 00:01:13.611
Your turn. Say: megastructure.

18
00:01:16.311 --> 00:01:17.452
megastructure.

19
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:19.788
post-car city.

20
00:01:20.488 --> 00:01:27.015
post-car city means a city designed for walking, cycling and transit instead of private cars.

21
00:01:27.315 --> 00:01:30.940
In a post-car city, streets become gardens.

22
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.546
Your turn. Say: post-car city.

23
00:01:38.246 --> 00:01:40.141
post-car city.

24
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.615
autonomous city.

25
00:01:43.315 --> 00:01:47.637
autonomous city means a city largely run by automated systems.

26
00:01:47.937 --> 00:01:51.875
In an autonomous city, buses and lights respond to sensors.

27
00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:55.855
Your turn. Say: autonomous city.

28
00:01:58.555 --> 00:01:59.834
autonomous city.

29
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.339
urban automation.

30
00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:08.301
urban automation means using machines and software to run city services.

31
00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:12.423
Urban automation could make rubbish collection silent and efficient.

32
00:02:13.123 --> 00:02:16.530
Your turn. Say: urban automation.

33
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.661
urban automation.

34
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.081
climate-proof city.

35
00:02:23.781 --> 00:02:27.591
climate-proof city means a city designed to withstand extreme weather.

36
00:02:27.891 --> 00:02:32.004
A climate-proof city stores floodwater and stays cool.

37
00:02:32.704 --> 00:02:37.342
Your turn. Say: climate-proof city.

38
00:02:40.042 --> 00:02:41.379
climate-proof city.

39
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:43.744
circular city.

40
00:02:44.444 --> 00:02:50.403
circular city means a city where waste becomes a resource and nothing is thrown away.

41
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:54.990
Amsterdam aims to become a circular city by 2050.

42
00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.259
Your turn. Say: circular city.

43
00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:03.216
circular city.

44
00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:06.483
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:10.025
Ms. Sarah, what will cities look like in a hundred years?

46
00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:16.985
Some visionaries dream of gleaming floating cities and towering arcologies.

47
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:19.830
That sounds exciting.

48
00:03:21.117 --> 00:03:27.229
Or alarming. 'Megastructure' can sound visionary or monstrous, depending on your view.

49
00:03:28.517 --> 00:03:32.815
I prefer the idea of a post-car city: quiet, green and walkable.

50
00:03:34.117 --> 00:03:37.463
A far cry from today's traffic-choked streets!

51
00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.458
But would an autonomous city be too controlled?

52
00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.353
There's a fine line between smart and surveilled.

53
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.902
Grammar focus: Collocation and connotation (advanced).

54
00:03:51.202 --> 00:03:55.059
Collocation and connotation shape a listener's attitude.

55
00:03:55.359 --> 00:04:00.586
Visionaries dream of gleaming floating cities and towering arcologies.

56
00:04:00.886 --> 00:04:03.780
There's a fine line between smart and surveilled.

57
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:11.559
Choose adjectives for connotation: 'ambitious' praises, 'grandiose' mocks, though both describe big plans.

58
00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.667
Now read and listen. Blueprints for Tomorrow.

59
00:04:15.967 --> 00:04:20.416
Architects and futurists have long imagined radically different cities.

60
00:04:21.717 --> 00:04:28.163
Paolo Soleri's arcology proposed enormous single structures that would combine housing, work and nature.

61
00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:35.292
Today, rising seas inspire designs for floating cities that could adapt to changing water levels.

62
00:04:36.583 --> 00:04:44.539
Some governments have announced vast megastructures, which critics call grandiose and supporters call visionary.

63
00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:54.432
Less dramatic ideas may prove more influential: the post-car city, the climate-proof city and the circular city.

64
00:04:55.717 --> 00:05:03.788
Urban automation could make services more efficient, and an autonomous city might respond instantly to traffic and pollution.

65
00:05:05.083 --> 00:05:11.413
Yet there is a fine line between convenience and control, especially when every movement is recorded.

66
00:05:12.717 --> 00:05:17.921
The cities of the future will reflect not only our technology but also our values.

67
00:05:19.217 --> 00:05:22.032
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:22.332 --> 00:05:25.388
A city where waste becomes a resource is a blank.

69
00:05:25.688 --> 00:05:30.409
A, circular city. B, floating city. C, megastructure.

70
00:05:34.417 --> 00:05:37.380
The answer is A: circular city.

71
00:05:38.883 --> 00:05:42.253
Which adjective has a negative connotation for big plans?

72
00:05:42.553 --> 00:05:46.926
A, grandiose. B, ambitious. C, visionary.

73
00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:53.381
The answer is A: grandiose.

74
00:05:54.883 --> 00:05:56.732
'A far cry from' means blank.

75
00:05:57.032 --> 00:06:01.417
A, very different from. B, close to. C, because of.

76
00:06:05.417 --> 00:06:08.148
The answer is A: very different from.

77
00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:13.705
A huge building containing a whole ecological city is an blank.

78
00:06:14.005 --> 00:06:19.748
A, arcology. B, autonomous city. C, post-car city.

79
00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.156
The answer is A: arcology.

80
00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.915
According to the text, who proposed the arcology?

81
00:06:31.215 --> 00:06:36.041
A, Paolo Soleri. B, Le Corbusier. C, Thomas More.

82
00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.804
The answer is A: Paolo Soleri.

83
00:06:44.317 --> 00:06:50.719
Now it's your turn. Part one: Describe your ideal city in 2100.

84
00:06:56.019 --> 00:07:03.406
Part two: Use two strong collocations and contrast a positive and a negative connotation.

85
00:07:09.706 --> 00:07:21.532
My ideal city would be a far cry from today's crowded streets. It would be a green, walkable, circular city. Smart technology would help, but there is a fine line between helpful and intrusive.

86
00:07:21.832 --> 00:07:24.366
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:30.882
Great work today! You learned eight new words and collocation and connotation (advanced).

88
00:07:31.182 --> 00:07:34.937
Today's value is wonder. Look up and wonder at the world.

89
00:07:35.237 --> 00:07:38.319
See you in Unit fifty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:38.619 --> 00:07:41.805
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

